insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d78c87b6-a0a8-50d7-8f3b-46367c0c07c7', 'rg7-story-details-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Folded Bridge$q$, $q$Keiko had tested her paper bridge eleven times, and eleven times it had sagged in the middle before the cup of pennies was even half full. The science fair was on Friday. Her notebook was a mess of crossed-out numbers and arrows pointing nowhere.

On Wednesday, her younger brother Haru wandered into the kitchen holding a paper fan he had folded in art class. He waved it in her face until she swatted it away. Then she stopped and took it from him.

She pressed the fan with her thumb. It barely bent. When she pressed a flat sheet of paper the same way, it folded in half at once.

Keiko spent the rest of the evening pleating strips of paper into tight zigzags and gluing them under the deck of a new bridge. Haru sat across the table and counted pennies into the cup, one at a time, calling out each number like a sports announcer.

At two hundred pennies, the old bridge would have collapsed. At three hundred, the new one still stood. At four hundred and twelve, it finally bowed, and the cup slid onto the table with a crash that made the dog bark.

On Friday, a judge named Mr. Adeyemi asked Keiko how she had come up with the folded supports. She pointed to the corner of her poster board, where a small paper fan was taped beside the title. Below it, in her neatest printing, was a single line: Design consultant, Haru Mori, age 7.$q$, 'draft')
