-- Passage rg7-story-details-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f757037d-f6ea-5b2c-9616-428cf205caa2', 'rg7-story-details-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-story-details-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Counting Sea Stars$q$, $q$Nia had pictured the tide pool survey as an adventure. Instead, she spent the first hour kneeling on cold rocks, holding a clipboard while Dr. Halvorsen lowered a square frame made of plastic pipe into the water.

"Count everything inside the square," Dr. Halvorsen said. "Then we move the square one length and count again."

The first square held four purple sea stars. So did the second. The third held two, and Nia wrote the number without looking up. Her fingers were stiff, and the wind kept flipping the page.

"Did you check under the ledge?" Dr. Halvorsen asked.

Nia had not. She leaned down, cupped her hand over her eyes to block the glare, and found three more sea stars pressed into the shadow beneath the rock. One of them was smaller than her thumbnail.

She erased the two and wrote a five. Then she looked back at the first square and the second.

"Can we go back?" she asked.

Dr. Halvorsen did not answer right away. She checked her watch and looked out at the water, which was creeping slowly up the rocks. "We have about forty minutes before the tide covers this row," she said. "Go quickly."

Nia went back. Under the ledge in the first square, she found two more. Under the second, she found one, and it was even tinier than the last.

When they finished the row, Dr. Halvorsen took the clipboard and read it carefully. She did not say "good job." She handed Nia the frame and said, "You can place it for the next row."$q$, 'draft')
